---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/omnisend-vs-attentive';
const title = 'Omnisend vs Attentive for Shopify SMS Marketing (2026)';
const desc = 'Compare Omnisend email, SMS and eligible web push with Attentive email/SMS journeys, account access, support scope and matched Shopify campaign tests.';
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Which should a Shopify store choose?",
    "answer": "Choose Omnisend when the team wants to build email, SMS and eligible web-push workflows together. Evaluate Attentive when its email/SMS journeys and quoted support scope address your operating needs. Prove the same merchant job in each account before selecting."
  },
  {
    "question": "Is Attentive only an SMS tool?",
    "answer": "No. Attentive currently offers email campaigns and triggered email alongside SMS, with other channels advertised too. Confirm the channels, integrations, permissions and services enabled in your proposed account."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does Omnisend push reach every shopper?",
    "answer": "No. Its current help center lists device and browser restrictions, including no iPhone web-push support. Push permission is separate from email and SMS permission; test the actual device mix before forecasting reach."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can push-only subscribers enter every automation?",
    "answer": "Do not assume so. Check the current trigger and identity requirements, test a push-only profile, and verify entry and delivery separately. A browser subscription does not establish an identified email or phone customer."
  },
  {
    "question": "Do I need another email platform?",
    "answer": "Neither comparison requires one. Both offer email. Retain a separate sender only for a demonstrated requirement, with explicit message ownership, suppression exchange and a tested retirement plan for overlapping jobs."
  },
  {
    "question": "Is there a revenue threshold for switching?",
    "answer": "No threshold is established here. Decide from verified workflow behavior, team access, support responsibilities and a matched quote, rather than a store revenue label."
  },
  {
    "question": "What must survive migration?",
    "answer": "Preserve channel-specific permission evidence, opt-outs, customer and order identifiers, segments, templates and audit history. Retire old queued sends and test purchase exits before activating replacement flows."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should costs be compared?",
    "answer": "Quote the same eligible audience, geography, email volume, SMS segments, peak month and service scope. Include carrier fees, add-ons, implementation, staff time and overlap, then compare incremental contribution against a holdout."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does tooling guarantee compliant sending?",
    "answer": "No. Assign an owner to validate capture disclosures, current purpose permission, recipient location rules, quiet hours and suppression behavior. Test opt-out handling and uncertain-location cases before launch."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="mb-8 text-sm"><a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a><span class="mx-2">/</span><span>Omnisend vs Attentive</span></nav>
      <header class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="badge">Shopify campaign operations</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Omnisend vs Attentive for Shopify SMS marketing</h1>
        <p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">Choose by the campaign your team can operate reliably: Omnisend combines email, SMS and eligible web push; Attentive offers email and SMS with a configurable commercial and support scope. Neither requires a second email tool by default.</p>
      </header>
      <div class="mt-12 max-w-4xl"><TldrBox picks={[
        { label: 'Omnisend', tool: 'Omnisend', reason: 'Test shared email/SMS/push workflow ownership and reachable devices' },
        { label: 'Attentive', tool: 'Attentive', reason: 'Test email/SMS journeys, operator access and the quoted service handoff' },
        { label: 'Decision gate', tool: 'Matched pilot', reason: 'Permission, order exits, inventory truth and contribution before expansion' },
      ]} /></div>
      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>What actually separates these options</h2>
        <p>Omnisend is a useful candidate when one team wants to edit several channels in one workflow. Its documentation separates channel subscription settings and warns that enabling an automation does not retire the corresponding Shopify workflow. Test publication, activation and replacement as separate operator actions. <a href="https://support.omnisend.com/en/articles/3954813-set-up-automation-workflow-settings">Omnisend workflow settings</a> describe these controls.</p>
        <p>Attentive is a useful candidate when the merchant values email/SMS orchestration plus a defined onboarding and support relationship. Its current pricing page describes modular channels and support rather than a universal store-size cutoff. Ask for a demonstration in the proposed account: who can edit, approve, publish, export and pause each journey; which tasks the merchant owns; and what the service team will deliver, by when. Put responsibilities, access, escalation and cancellation terms in the agreement. See <a href="https://www.attentive.com/pricing">Attentive pricing and scope</a>.</p>
        <h2>Web push is an eligibility decision</h2>
        <p>Omnisend's <a href="https://support.omnisend.com/en/articles/15082284-push-notification-channel-faq">current push FAQ</a> lists supported browser/device combinations and excludes iPhone web push. This is the product's documented eligibility, not a claim about every browser implementation. Measure eligible visitors with permission rather than treating the whole email list as push reach. That FAQ also describes browser-specific contacts and potential duplicates after signup forms: test checkout identity merging separately from form capture.</p>
        <p>For automation, test a push-only subscriber before attaching a sales forecast. A workflow accepting a push message does not establish that every anonymous browser can enter its trigger. Use <a href="https://support.omnisend.com/en/articles/2608314-get-started-with-push-notification-channel">push setup guidance</a> and the actual account to verify trigger eligibility, identity, permission and delivery. Never turn failed push delivery into an SMS fallback without current SMS permission for that purpose.</p>
        <h2>Proposed merchant fixtures: drops and inventory</h2>
        <p>These are fictional acceptance tests derived from merchant jobs, not customer evidence or predicted outcomes. Run the same job in both accounts and record what is native, configured, manual or unavailable.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Loom &amp; Frame:</strong> disclose the print edition size, then test a halfway-sold notice for identified repeat collectors. Read sellable inventory immediately before dispatch; cancel after purchase or sellout.</li>
          <li><strong>Sauce Lab:</strong> propose one launch notice and one low-stock notice. Require a verified bottle count and retire the second job if its threshold is no longer true.</li>
          <li><strong>Ember Ceramics:</strong> use a kiln-release waitlist with timestamped priority. Invitations must not promise stock reservations unless the commerce system actually reserves units.</li>
          <li><strong>SoleDrop ATX:</strong> match sneaker waitlists to size-level stock. A restocked size should not wake every profile, and duplicate inventory events must not send duplicate invitations.</li>
          <li><strong>Driftwood Surf Co:</strong> separate seasonal surf content from a requested restock alert. Recheck size, availability and the alert's current purpose permission before sending.</li>
        </ul>
        <h2>Proposed merchant fixtures: audience and offers</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Ridge Run Coffee:</strong> exclude active subscribers from acquisition flash offers using current subscription status. Test a cancellation and reactivation arriving out of order.</li>
          <li><strong>Cipher Audio:</strong> offer launch education by email and a permitted browse-interest reminder without an automatic discount. Compare incremental contribution with an eligible holdout.</li>
          <li><strong>Patchwork Denim:</strong> match one-of-one listings to declared style preferences. A sold item must exit every pending reminder, even if the shopper remains in the style segment.</li>
          <li><strong>Arena Energy:</strong> keep flavor-interest targeting distinct from athlete content engagement. If email includes a gift, show SMS recipients the actual eligibility rather than implying a different offer.</li>
          <li><strong>Peak Trail Gear:</strong> test clearance offers against a holdout among eligible bargain shoppers. Keep full-price loyalists separate; do not assume a discount creates incremental demand.</li>
        </ul>
        <h2>Proposed merchant fixtures: calendar and customer promises</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Noir Collective:</strong> schedule VIP access before general release, with an explicit timezone and access expiry. Enforce one customer-level campaign budget across email and SMS.</li>
          <li><strong>Festival Threads Co:</strong> use requested city interests and venue dates. A city change must retire old-location jobs; store timezone alone is insufficient for recipient scheduling.</li>
          <li><strong>Vinyl Vault Records:</strong> combine pressing interests with verified collector order history. Specify how refunds affect value tiers and do not infer genre preference from spend alone.</li>
          <li><strong>Bloom &amp; Wick:</strong> separate gifting deadlines from self-purchase education. Recheck destination shipping eligibility and stop deadline messages when the delivery promise expires.</li>
        </ul>
        <h2>Permission, customer identity and order truth</h2>
        <p>For each fixture, retain capture source, time, disclosure version, channel, purpose and subsequent withdrawal. A historical phone number or browser token is not current promotional permission. Verify applicable location and quiet-hour controls with the responsible reviewer; unresolved location or permission should hold a job for review. Test STOP, resubscription and help routing on the real sender configuration.</p>
        <p>Keep Shopify customer, checkout and order identifiers alongside each platform profile. Test guest checkout, changed email, shared phone, browser-only capture and merged profiles. Reconcile against the commerce order ledger rather than a campaign attribution report. Define how paid, canceled, refunded and partially returned orders affect eligibility and value tiers; replay delayed events to prove they cannot undo a newer state.</p>
        <h2>Purchase exits and queued-job retirement</h2>
        <p>Place an order while a cart reminder is delayed and while a broadcast is queued. Confirm both channels stop the inappropriate message, including after a profile merge. Segment refresh at entry is not proof of a final send check. Record event receipt, eligibility evaluation and send timestamps; pause the pilot if stale eligibility can reach dispatch.</p>
        <p>Assign a stable job key from customer, campaign, event and channel. Retry a webhook and a failed dispatch without creating another message; reconcile ambiguous delivery responses before retrying. During migration, inventory active and delayed jobs in Shopify and both senders, cancel or drain the old queue, and save evidence of retirement before enabling replacements. Rollback must pause the new sender first and reconcile already-sent keys before restoring the old flow.</p>
        <h2>Approvals and the operating handoff</h2>
        <p>The merchant signs off audience, inventory, offer and customer promise. The messaging owner signs off channel permission, suppression, quiet hours and collision rules. The account or service owner demonstrates who can publish and pause, exports the job inventory, and provides escalation contacts. Test an urgent stop outside the usual operator's hours. A service promise is useful only when the contract and working account make the responsibility concrete.</p>
        <h2>Matched cost and contribution worksheet</h2>
        <p>Use the same geography, eligible audience, SMS encoding and segment counts, email sends, peak-month volume and support requirements in both quotes. Include contact billing, carrier charges, add-ons, setup, staff time and migration overlap. Check <a href="https://www.omnisend.com/pricing/">Omnisend pricing</a> and the Attentive quote at decision time; no fixed price or revenue floor is assumed here.</p>
        <p>Hypothetical test, not vendor performance: randomly allocate 1,000 eligible customers to treatment and 1,000 to holdout for the same window. Treatment net sales of $5,000 less $2,000 product cost, $500 fulfillment, $100 support and $200 messaging/platform allocation leave $2,200 contribution. Holdout net sales of $4,200 less $1,680 product cost, $420 fulfillment and $80 support leave $2,020. Incremental contribution is $180, or $0.18 per eligible treatment customer. Net sales already deduct discounts and returns; do not subtract them twice. Allocate fixed costs consistently and account for implementation before extrapolating.</p>
        <h2>Decision after the pilot</h2>
        <p>Prefer Omnisend if the tested multi-channel workflow and eligible push audience simplify this team's work at the matched cost. Prefer Attentive if its tested email/SMS behavior, account access and contracted support improve execution enough to justify its quote. Keep the current system if neither clears permission, order-exit and contribution gates. More channels or a larger contract are not an automatic upgrade.</p>
        <p>Continue with <a href="/apps/omnisend">Omnisend</a>, <a href="/apps/attentive">Attentive</a>, <a href="/guides/consent">consent capture</a>, <a href="/guides/pricing">pricing economics</a> and <a href="/guides/campaign-restraint">campaign restraint</a>.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-16"><FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Omnisend vs Attentive FAQ" /></section>
</BaseLayout>
